there's a semblance of calm on the  streets of the ethiopian capital ali  sababa but this is a city fearing the  prelude to a storm which is gathering on  its doorstep  draped in the colors of the ethiopian  flag ephraim solomon popularly known as  ethiopia's flagman is out preaching  patriotism to a nation divided by  conflict  in these uncertain times of conflict  ephraim preaches about unity and an end  to the deeply entrained tribalism in  ethiopia  i have not had good sleep for the past  three months i keep worrying about the  conflict i therefore decided to do my  bit and preach about cohesion to my  people the ethiopian government insists  the capital and its bustling markets are  safe it has also been rallying residents  to take up arms as a last-ditch defense  against the rebels from tigray who say  they have every intention to match onto  the capital  addis ababa is safe the government has  stepped up security and i don't think  those rebels will have any chance  reaching here  the tigray conflict had a huge impact on  ethiopia's economy which until now was  one of the fastest growing in africa  this is ethiopia's wall street the heart  of adi sababa's business district  traders say the conflict in tigray and  the covet 19 pandemic have had a  devastating effect on businesses here  it's not clear how much the war has cost  but economic analysts predict that  ethiopia's defense expenditure is set to  rise to 502 million dollars  up from 460 million last year  overall economic growth is also set to  significantly reduce from six percent  last year to just two percent this year  the lowest in more than two decades  according to the international monetary  fund  just two years ago ethiopian prime  minister abi ahmed was praised by the  international community he was awarded  the nobel peace prize for ending the  conflict with neighboring eritrea  but his time in office has been largely  defined by conflict his government  declared a state of emergency as the  